problems.problem:
Prove that if positive integers $a,b,c$ satisfy $a^2+b^2=c^2$, then:

(1) at least one of $a$, $b$ is divisible by $3$;

(2) at least one of $a$, $b$ is divisible by $4$;

(3) at least one of $a$, $b$, $c$ is divisible by $5$.

Consider a set of $n>3$ points in the plane, no three collinear, and a positive integer $k<n$. Prove that:

(a) if $k\leq\frac{n}{2}$, each point can be joined by segments to at least $k$ other points so that no three segments form a triangle;

(b) if $k>\frac{n}{2}$ and each point is joined by segments to at least $k$ other points, then some three segments form a triangle.

A regular tetrahedron with edge $1$ is given. The distance between two points of its surface $S$ is the length of the shortest polygonal line on $S$ joining them. Prove that:

(a) there are four points of $S$ such that every point of the surface is at distance at most $\frac{1}{2}$ from one of them;

(b) there are no three points with this property.

A polyhedron $W$ has the following properties:

(i) it has a centre of symmetry;

(ii) the section of $W$ by any plane through the centre of symmetry and an edge of $W$ is a parallelogram;

(iii) some vertex of $W$ belongs to exactly three edges.

Prove that $W$ is a parallelepiped.

Let $V$ be the set of all vectors with integer coordinates in the Cartesian plane. Find all functions $f:V\to\mathbb{R}$ such that:

(i) $f(v)=1$ for each of the four vectors $v\in V$ of unit length;

(ii) $f(v+w)=f(v)+f(w)$ for any two perpendicular vectors $v,w\in V$ (the zero vector is perpendicular to every vector).
